<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Welf VI</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1115">1115</a>–<a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Welf VI was a German prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf dynasty</a> who held the Matildine lands, the March of Tuscany and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> from 1152, granted by his nephew Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. He was also an uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> and ruled the family's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. He granted privileges to Tuscan towns such as Lucca, and after the death of his only son in 1167 he sold his inheritance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/welf-vi.jpg" alt="Welf VI" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wgt_Stifterb%C3%BCchlein_38r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Welf VI was the third son of Henry IX, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He inherited the family's possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, including the counties of Altdorf and Ravensburg, while his eldest brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-proud">Henry the Proud</a> received the duchies of Bavaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and his brother Conrad entered the church. Henry married him to Uta, daughter of Godfrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calw">Calw</a>, count palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and on Godfrey's death in 1131 Welf disputed the inheritance of Calw with Godfrey's nephew Adalbert. His sister Judith was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, who was at most a decade younger than his uncle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-of-germany">Conrad III of Germany</a> confiscated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1142, Welf rebelled. Although he and Conrad went on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> together, Welf and his son Welf VII were defeated by Conrad's son Henry Berengar at the Battle of Flochberg. Peace came in 1152, when Barbarossa was elected king, and in October that year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> the new king gave Welf, as head of his house, the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, the margraviate of Tuscany and the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> among other Italian lands.</p><p>From the 1150s father and son also fought a feud with Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, count palatine of Swabia, which peaked between 1164 and 1166 and was settled by the emperor, largely in favour of Welf and his son. In 1167 Welf VII died of malaria at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while campaigning with Barbarossa against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>. Henry the Lion claimed all the Welf estates; Welf asked a large sum for them, which Henry did not raise, and the Italian states went to the emperor, though Welf stayed in charge of the Italian duchies until 1173.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> was stripped of his estates in 1179, the Welf lands Henry had acquired returned to Welf, and at his death on 15 December 1191 they passed to Barbarossa's heir, the duke of Swabia. So the Swabian Welf lands went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, while the male line descended from Henry the Lion kept its lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Welf was a patron of churches and was buried in Steingaden Abbey, the Premonstratensian house he founded, beside his son. He was also the patron of the Historia Welforum, the first medieval chronicle of his dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Welf VI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1160 Duke <b>Welf VI</b> granted the city wide rights, confirming its self-government over the surrounding district.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Republic of Lucca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Memmingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.99,10.18&amp;year=1164" aria-label="Memmingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Welf VI"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 5975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 5975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.99,10.18&amp;year=1164">What was here in 1164? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
